## University application coding

University application records are collapsed to one record per individual and calendar year. If an individual applied to several university study places in the same year, the annual outcome is coded as admission if the individual was admitted to at least one of them; otherwise the annual outcome is rejection.

In the preparation code, `child_shnro` identifies the individual and `vuosi` identifies the calendar year.

- `access = .` indicates no university application in that year;
- `access = 0` indicates that the individual applied but was not admitted;
- `access = 1` indicates that the individual was admitted to at least one university study place.

From this, `hakee` identifies whether the individual applied and `paasee` identifies university admission. Years with no application are retained because they are needed to identify stop applying during longitudinal follow-up.


## Construction of the longitudinal person-year sample

The analysis of **stop applying after rejection** is longitudinal and restricted to the 2,797 initially rejected applicants. Annual records are organized as person-years. The manuscript reports 4,987 person-years in these models.

The first university application defines the beginning of the application history. The re-application analysis includes individuals whose first application ended in rejection. Follow-up begins in the next calendar year.

`hakukerta` is the annual clock from the first application. The first application year is `hakukerta = 1`, so `hakukerta = 2` is the first post-rejection risk interval. The models include `hakukerta` as a categorical time variable to allow the baseline hazard to vary across annual intervals. Follow-up is limited to a maximum of four post-rejection years.

The outcome `event` is coded 1 in the first follow-up year in which the individual does not apply to university and 0 otherwise while the individual remains at risk. A gap year therefore counts as stop applying.

Follow-up ends at the earliest of:

- stop applying;
- university admission after rejection;
- four post-rejection follow-up years; or
- the last observable year.

Years in which individuals are older than 24 are excluded before the risk set is constructed. Admission after rejection ends follow-up with `event = 0` in the admission year.


